    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                You're building a wheelchair ramp from the ground to a patio that is 3 feet above the ground. According to the Americans with Disabilities Act it must have a  ratio of 1:12 maximum slope, or one foot in elevation change for every 12 feet along the ground. 

            </p>
  

        </introduction>


        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                  Draw a right triangle that models the ramp you will build to 3 feet above the ground and label the two legs with information given in the problem.
                </p>

            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                  Find the (minimum) length of the ramp.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the angle (in degrees) that the ramp will make with the ground. (Use an inverse trig function!)
                           </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                   <url href="https://www.braunability.com/us/en/blog/disability-rights/wheelchair-ramp-slope.html#">This website</url>
                                      says ``The commercial and public facility standard for slope is 1:12 (in inches)
                                      or about 5 degrees of incline.'' Is that accurate?
                                      If a builder built a ramp with a 5 degree incline, would it be ADA compliant?
                              </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              The same website says ``For those building ramps at home, the ratio can be as steep as 3:12." What angle will the ramp make with the ground if complying with this slope?
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>

        

    </exercise>
</worksheet>